---
import Adr from "../components/Adr.astro";
import Diagram from "../components/Diagram.astro";
import Excerpt from "../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import Path from "../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Tree from "../components/Tree.astro";

const CHART = "deploy/helm/iki";
const TPL = "deploy/config-template";
const PUB = ".github/workflows/publish.yml";
const REL = ".github/workflows/release.yml";

const boxes = [
  { id: "source", x: 20, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Source", sub: "Wasm as source + SHA-256" },
  { id: "deps", x: 250, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Dependency gate", sub: "3 days old; licences" },
  { id: "live", x: 480, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Live-test gate", sub: "every cloud, the tag" },
  { id: "publish", x: 710, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "publish.yml", sub: "7 images, signed", strong: true },
  { id: "release", x: 710, y: 150, w: 190, h: 60, label: "release.yml", sub: "chart, worker, npm" },
  { id: "config", x: 20, y: 150, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Config repository", sub: "one per deployment" },
  { id: "build", x: 250, y: 150, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Deployment build", sub: "build, sign, approve", strong: true },
  { id: "customer", x: 480, y: 150, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Customer registry", sub: "licensed images" },
  { id: "infra", x: 20, y: 270, w: 190, h: 60, label: "OpenTofu", sub: "GCP and AWS roots" },
  { id: "cluster", x: 250, y: 270, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Cluster", sub: "one Helm chart", strong: true },
  { id: "admit", x: 480, y: 270, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Admission", sub: "signed or approved" },
  { id: "workers", x: 710, y: 270, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Lab workers", sub: "update to signed releases" },
];
const edges = [
  { from: "source", to: "deps" },
  { from: "deps", to: "live" },
  { from: "live", to: "publish" },
  { from: "publish", to: "release", dashed: true },
  { from: "publish", to: "build", label: "base images, checked" },
  { from: "config", to: "build" },
  { from: "customer", to: "build" },
  { from: "build", to: "cluster", label: "Deploy PR, GitOps" },
  { from: "infra", to: "cluster" },
  { from: "cluster", to: "admit" },
  { from: "release", to: "workers" },
];

const H = {
  short: "How Iki gets from this repository to a lab's cluster",
  how: "From a tagged release to admitted pods",
  example: "A release reaching a lab's staging deployment, with a licensed image",
  rules: "The chart, infrastructure, images, licensed images, dependencies and Wasm",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Each organization gets its own deployment on Kubernetes, from one Helm chart. Iki publishes
      signed images with provenance and SBOMs, but only after a live deployment to every cloud has
      passed for the tag. A deployment is a git repository of its own: its CI builds the pinned
      plugins onto Iki's images, signs the result, approves the images its runs may use, and opens a
      pull request whose merge is the deploy. In the cluster, run pods are admitted only if Iki
      signed their image or the deployment approved it. Reference OpenTofu sets up Google Cloud or
      AWS around the chart.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Single-tenant deployments on Kubernetes, with Helm (<Adr n={[2, 14]} />).</li>
      <li>GitOps configuration per deployment (<Adr n="0015" />).</li>
      <li>Signed, attested artifacts verified at deploy time (<Adr n={[17, 63]} />).</li>
      <li>Deployments supply licensed images Iki doesn't ship (<Adr n="0209" />).</li>
    </ul>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <Diagram id="deploy" title="From Iki's release to a lab's cluster" width={920} height={350} boxes={boxes} edges={edges}>
      <div slot="source">
        <p>
          No <code>.wasm</code> file is ever in git: the Rust source and a SHA-256 for each module are,
          and builds check the module they make against it.
        </p>
        <p><Path p="spec/tests/test_build_backend.py" sym="def test_iki_wasm_is_only_ever_built(" />, <Path p="server/iki_wasm.wasm.sha256" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="deps">
        <p>
          pnpm and uv refuse releases younger than three days; a script checks the rest of the locks.
          OSV checks vulnerabilities and licences, cargo-deny the worker's crates (<Adr n={[17, 19]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="scripts/supply-chain/check-deps.sh" />, <Path p="scripts/supply-chain/release-age.py" sym="MINIMUM_AGE = timedelta(days=3)" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="live">
        <p>Both the publish and release workflows refuse a tag until each cloud's live test has passed at it.</p>
        <p><Path p="deploy/live/live_runs.py" />, <Path p="deploy/live-runs.json" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="publish">
        <p>
          Seven images for amd64 and arm64: the server, the worker and five first-party tool images.
          Each is scanned with Grype, signed keyless with cosign, and given SLSA provenance and a
          CycloneDX SBOM per platform, then verified in the same run (<Adr n={[17, 65]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={PUB} sym='run: cosign sign --yes "$REF"' /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="release">
        <p>
          The chart as an OCI artifact, signed; the worker's tarballs with Sigstore bundles; and the
          <code>@ikisci/science</code> and <code>@ikisci/ui</code> packages with npm provenance (<Adr
            n="0017"
          />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={REL} sym="helm package deploy/helm/iki" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="config">
        <p>
          One folder per deployment: the Iki version and images by digest, the plugin lockfile,
          chart values, settings, and the image file CI writes (<Adr n={[15, 34]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={`${TPL}/deployments/staging/iki.yaml`} />, <Path p={`${TPL}/README.md`} /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="build">
        <p>
          Every pull request runs <code>iki deploy build</code>. On merge, CI checks Iki's signatures
          on the base images, builds and signs the deployment's server and worker images, signs each
          image runs will use into an approvals package, and opens a "Deploy" pull request (<Adr
            n={[34, 63]}
          />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={`${TPL}/.github/workflows/build.yml`} />, <Path p="deploy/plugins.Dockerfile" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="customer">
        <p>
          A licensed image the lab supplies, bound to an environment and a range of plugin releases,
          pinned by digest, with an optional pull Secret (<Adr n="0209" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/compute.py" sym="def image_binding(" />, <Path p="cli/src/iki_cli/deploy.py" sym="def run_images(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="infra">
        <p>
          Reference OpenTofu for Google Cloud and AWS: network, cluster, database, storage, secrets,
          access, the VM tier and more, with an output of chart values (<Adr n={[97, 107, 109]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="deploy/gcp/production" />, <Path p="deploy/aws/production" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="cluster">
        <p>
          The web pods (which also serve MCP), the task runner, a migrate hook before every install,
          upgrade and rollback, backend workers, the provisioner, the egress proxy, and network
          policies that deny everything by default (<Adr n={[14, 53, 155]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={`${CHART}/templates`} />, <Path p={`${CHART}/values.yaml`} /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="admit">
        <p>
          A Sigstore policy on the pods the provisioner makes: Iki's publish workflow or the
          deployment's approvals. On Google Cloud, Binary Authorization denies every other image
          cluster-wide (<Adr n={[63, 107]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={`${CHART}/templates/image-policy.yaml`} sym="kind: ClusterImagePolicy" />, <Path p="deploy/gcp/modules/binary-authorization/main.tf" sym="ALWAYS_DENY" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="workers">
        <p>
          Workers on people's computers update themselves, verifying offline that the release workflow
          signed the new version. VM-tier machines never update themselves (<Adr n="0060" />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/update.rs" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/machines.py" sym='"IKI_WORKER_AUTO_UPDATE": "0"' /></p>
      </div>
    </Diagram>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      Iki tags a release after both clouds' live tests pass; <code>publish.yml</code> signs its images
      and <code>release.yml</code> its chart. A lab's staging deployment moves to it with one pull
      request to its config repository, which starts from this template:
    </p>
    <Tree dir={`${TPL}/deployments/staging`} depth={1} />
    <Excerpt file={`${TPL}/deployments/staging/iki.yaml`} start="^serverImage:" />
    <p>
      The lab also runs its own single-cell plugin, whose analysis needs a vendor's licensed image that Iki can't ship. Its
      environment declares <code>customer_image_required: true</code>, and the lab's Compute admin
      binds it, in the shape <Adr n="0209" /> gives:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="docs/adr/0209-deployments-supply-licensed-oci-images.md" start="^kind: EnvironmentImageBinding" note="from ADR 0209's example" />
    <p>
      The pull request's build lists that image with the others runs use. On merge, CI signs it into
      the deployment's approvals package, which the chart's image policy trusts:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={`${CHART}/values.yaml`} start="^  imagePolicy:" />
    <p>
      Merging the Deploy pull request lets Argo CD or Flux apply the chart. The migrate hook runs
      first. A run of the licensed action pins the bound image when it's queued, so retries keep it,
      and the provisioner's pod carries the pull Secret. On Google Cloud the registry path must also
      be allowed by Binary Authorization, or the pod is refused there.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="The chart" open>
        <p>
          One chart for every environment, requiring Kubernetes 1.33 or later. Templates render by
          value: the provisioner when a container or VM tier is on, the image policy with the
          container tier and <code>imagePolicy.enabled</code>, the egress proxy and network policies
          by default (<Adr n="0014" />).
        </p>
        <Tree dir={`${CHART}/templates`} depth={1} />
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Infrastructure">
        <p>
          On Google Cloud, Artifact Registry tags are immutable, files use Cloud Storage's own API,
          and Binary Authorization allows only the deployment's repositories, Iki's, and listed
          extras. On AWS, pods get credentials through EKS Pod Identity. Both production roots take a
          <code>recovery</code> variable for rebuilding in another region (<Adr n={[97, 100, 107, 109]} />).
        </p>
        <Tree dir="deploy/gcp/modules" depth={1} />
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Licensed customer images">
        <p>
          A provisioned environment needs an image pinned by digest or the customer flag. Exactly one
          binding may match an installed plugin version; overlapping bindings and a pull Secret on the
          wrong kind of template fail the deployment build. An action with no binding is unavailable,
          and its launch refusal says which environment needs one (<Adr n="0209" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/runs/catalog.py"
            sym="def customer_image("
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/kubernetes.py" sym='pod["imagePullSecrets"]' />).
          Compute admins see each environment that needs one, the binding its installed version
          matches, and bindings that match nothing, under Compute → Images; the page is read-only,
          since bindings change only in <code>compute.yaml</code> (<Path
            p="web/app/src/pages/compute-images.tsx"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/compute.py" sym="def list_image_bindings(" />). No
          first-party plugin needs one yet.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Dependencies">
        <p>
          The three-day minimum age is set in pnpm and uv; for Cargo, which has no such setting, a
          script checks the lock. The licence allowlist is a file of its own; per-package exceptions,
          such as psycopg's LGPL, live beside the vulnerability exceptions, and each vulnerability
          exception expires on a date. Dependabot waits seven days (<Adr n="0019" />).
        </p>
        <Excerpt file="pnpm-workspace.yaml" start="^minimumReleaseAge:" />
        <p><Path p="scripts/supply-chain/allowed-licenses.txt" />, <Path p="osv-scanner.toml" />, <Path p="worker/deny.toml" />, <Path p=".github/dependabot.yml" /></p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Wasm modules">
        <p>
          The server's Wasm is built once, in a reusable workflow, and checked against the pinned
          digests before it's published. A worker bundle refuses a module whose digest doesn't match
          (<Path p=".github/workflows/build-server-wasm.yml" />, <Path
            p="cli/tests/test_workers_bundle.py"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The dev cluster">
        <p>
          kind with kindnet, so the chart's network policies are enforced locally, and CloudNativePG
          pinned by digest. The same chart runs there (<Adr n={[14, 22]} />, <Path
            p="deploy/dev/kind-config.yaml"
          />, <Path p="deploy/dev/up.sh" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0014" /> lists mcp and scheduler deployments; MCP is a route on the web pods, and
            scheduled work is the task runner and a maintenance CronJob. It also names IRSA (the code
            uses Pod Identity) and MinIO (replaced by SeaweedFS in <Adr n="0022" />).
          </li>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0107" /> says files go through Cloud Storage's S3-compatible API; <Adr n="0109" />
            and the code use its own API, and 0107's status doesn't say so.
          </li>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0017" /> says each artifact ships an SBOM; the chart doesn't.
          </li>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0063" />, the chart's comments and the supply-chain doc say Iki's images are signed
            by its release workflow; <code>publish.yml</code> signs them, and that's what the policy
            pins.
          </li>
          <li>
            The config template pushes a deployment's images to ghcr, but the Google Cloud
            reference's Binary Authorization allows only its Artifact Registry and Iki's images, so
            those would be refused unless listed as extras.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={CHART} />: the chart</li>
        <li><Path p={TPL} />: a deployment's config repository</li>
        <li><Path p="deploy/gcp" />, <Path p="deploy/aws" />: reference infrastructure</li>
        <li><Path p={PUB} />, <Path p={REL} />: images and releases</li>
        <li><Path p="docs/supply-chain.md" />: verifying what Iki publishes</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0014" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0015" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0017" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0034" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0060" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0063" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0097" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0107" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0209" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
